ay so how high are the stakes  like is this the last battle of the  movie or is it like the first battle you  know so you can kind of know how much to  give  but the last one when he died was like  oh my god yeah i've watched that  that uh sequence over and over and over  again and it gets me every single time  i know and at the time too i was like  but there's hope  right i mean like he's not totally dead  and they kind of looked at me like  i thought oh my god i knew my gravy  train had just come to a halt  and so they said do it like it's the  saddest thing  say it like it's the saddest thing  you've ever said and i was like well  that won't be [ __ ] hard like he's  just  like it's all over that's the one had to  go there yeah that was it was such a  great opportunity really was  i i adore that franchise quite a bit and  i'm i'm eager to see it grow even more  the next one for you better call saul  for this one i was  reading something that you said about  the process and just in terms of it  being a learning curve for you  so now i'm wondering for anybody else  out there who might book a super cool  show like that  and get to jump in for just a handful of  episodes what advice would you give them  to  make the most of the experience on the  role oh  that's a good question too um i would  say  be completely prepared and not nervous  because like they just don't have time  for somebody being nervous  like you just i'm glad it came at that  point in my career to be honest with you  because if it had come early in my  career i think i would have over thought  it and i would have been very nervous  but like jonathan is very strong person  and he's very prepared and he's  like ready to do it right and take one  there's no like warming up and  so i would say just be very confident in  your choices and be prepared and don't  be nervous  because tv goes very quickly and they  don't have time for ten  takes and they don't have time for you  making mistakes  so you have to be able to cool as a  breeze come in and i guess  saying not be nervous is because it's  not really about  my character the story is about  jonathan's character  and so i think maybe swallow your ego a  little bit and remember  that you're facilitating the story of a  main character you're not the main  character  so i have to kind of remember that  how can i make jonathan's job as easy as  possible  and as stress-free as possible because  he's going to be working when i'm going  home  he's got more days to do and he's older  than me too and  there has to be like a respect for an  older actor i think  um as there should be obviously he's got  more years more experience on me  so make the person that you're working  with who's the lead  their job as easy as possible and if  that means like shutting up in the  makeup trailer not saying anything  then shut up in the makeup trailer and  don't say anything like whatever they  want  you give and that's your job i think  with that in mind beyond just maybe not  talking in the makeup trailer  what is something that you could do for  him or you've done for him  on set as a scene partner that you hope  one day someone does for you  well he's pretty easy to work with now  to be honest with you you don't have to  help him that much like  i help him but i mean i chat to him in  between takes and things like that you  know  um let me think what have i done for him  that i hope somebody would do for me  well there you go just that  know your lines and know your lines and  don't be taken up all the time and  thinking of wanting to think about your  character very  much like again be prepared  come in and deliver something really  great that somebody can work off of  and remember it's not about you it's not  your show  that's i think i that's when it works  best  too i think is when you feel like you're  partners as opposed to one person wants  to overtake the scene it's kind of  that's when it becomes a bit like  well it's a dance really between the two  of us so  absolutely that's a hard one i  can't think of anything amazing i've  done from to be honest  i'm sure i'm sure he's aware of it i  could probably ask him this question and  he'll give me a laundry list of things  one day  oh he probably tells you such a gent  so i got one more for you before we go  into dreamland because  everyone's going kind of crazy right now  over  fincher because we're all really looking  forward to mank and i know things didn't  pan out on the show  that you worked with him on but is there  anything about your experience working  with david fincher that  i guess wowed you were something that  was super unique about his process with  working with actors  oh my gosh i'm so glad you brought that  up thank you first of all like i've  always wanted to work with him because i  heard stories that he does loads of  takes  and i was like i would do billions of  takes for just the sake of being in the  room with david fincher  i am such a big fan of his work  and i like to try different things and  different experiences  so yeah no joke he does  so many takes i had a scene where i had  to smoke a cigarette for every take  on a phone call and we did 76 takes  so i must have smoked i think i smoked  some real ones sometimes just because i  was like i need a real cigarette  and take like 50 or something but then  it would be herbals  and then real ones and herbals and i  think  uh i loved it i would do it again in a  heartbeat  i loved every minute of it and it didn't  feel like  um in the beginning i was a little  worried that like  oh is it because i did it wrong or am i  really [ __ ] is that  why we're on take 27 because like this  is not good like what am i doing wrong  and then you realize no it's not that  he's just gonna keep going just keep  going it's nothing  just keep doing it keep offering  different stuff and just go with it  and it starts to become kind of surreal  because you get your appointed like take